<h1>CHAPTER THREE</h1>
<h1>SYSTEM ANALYSIS AND METHODOLOGY</h1>

<h2>3.1 Introduction</h2>
<p>This chapter describes the methods, technologies, and procedures used to design and develop the Smart Truck Dispatch and Cargo Marketplace Management System. It explains the development methodology, data-collection techniques, system requirements, architecture, database design, security controls, testing methods, and ethical considerations. The system connects customers who require cargo transportation with registered truck drivers while enabling administrators to verify users and vehicles, supervise bookings, monitor trips, and generate reports.</p>

<h2>3.2 Research Methodology</h2>
<p>The project used an applied research approach because it aims to solve practical problems in cargo transportation. These problems include difficulty finding suitable trucks, manual dispatch procedures, poor communication between customers and drivers, and limited visibility into shipment progress. A qualitative approach was used to understand the activities and challenges experienced by customers, drivers, and transport administrators. The collected information was converted into functional and non-functional system requirements.</p>

<h2>3.3 Data Collection Methods</h2>
<h3>3.3.1 Interviews</h3>
<p>Semi-structured interviews were conducted with truck drivers, customers, fleet owners, and transport administrators. The interviews focused on truck registration, cargo booking, pricing, driver selection, trip management, payment recording, and common operational challenges.</p>
<h3>3.3.2 Observation</h3>
<p>Existing cargo transportation processes were observed to understand how customers locate drivers, negotiate services, confirm truck availability, and monitor deliveries. The observation showed that customers depend heavily on telephone calls and personal contacts, booking information is not consistently recorded, and administrators cannot easily prepare operational reports.</p>
<h3>3.3.3 Document Review</h3>
<p>Relevant records were reviewed, including driver information, vehicle-registration documents, driving licences, cargo details, booking records, and trip-status reports. This helped identify the data that the proposed system needed to collect and manage.</p>

<h2>3.4 Analysis of the Existing System</h2>
<p>The existing cargo-transport process is mainly manual. Customers normally contact drivers or transport agents by telephone, explain the cargo type, weight, pickup location, and destination, and then negotiate availability and price.</p>
<p>The existing approach has the following limitations:</p>
<ol>
  <li>Customers cannot easily compare available trucks.</li>
  <li>Driver and vehicle documents may not be properly verified.</li>
  <li>Booking information can be lost or misunderstood.</li>
  <li>Customers have limited shipment-tracking information.</li>
  <li>Drivers experience difficulty finding suitable cargo.</li>
  <li>Administrators lack centralized operational information.</li>
  <li>Report preparation requires significant manual work.</li>
</ol>

<h2>3.5 Proposed System</h2>
<p>The proposed system is a web-based platform connecting customers, truck drivers, and administrators. Customers can register, browse eligible FTL trucks, view open shared-load trips, submit cargo requests, review driver bids, book transportation, and monitor shipments. Drivers can register their vehicles, upload verification documents, update availability, bid on cargo requests, create shared-load trips, and update delivery progress. Administrators can verify drivers, manage users and trucks, monitor bookings and trips, review payments, and generate reports.</p>

<h2>3.6 Development Methodology</h2>
<p>The Agile development methodology was selected for this project. Agile divides development into small, manageable iterations. Each iteration delivers a functional part of the system that can be reviewed, tested, and improved.</p>
<p>The project followed these stages:</p>
<ol>
  <li><b>Requirement collection:</b> Information was gathered from users and existing transport procedures.</li>
  <li><b>Requirement analysis:</b> Collected information was converted into system requirements.</li>
  <li><b>System design:</b> Interfaces, database tables, roles, and architecture were designed.</li>
  <li><b>Implementation:</b> Frontend and backend components were developed.</li>
  <li><b>Testing:</b> Individual modules and complete workflows were tested.</li>
  <li><b>Evaluation:</b> System functionality was compared with user requirements.</li>
  <li><b>Improvement:</b> Identified errors and usability issues were corrected.</li>
</ol>

<h2>3.7 System Requirements</h2>
<h3>3.7.1 Functional Requirements</h3>
<p><b>User authentication:</b> The system shall allow registration and secure login, provide password recovery, enforce role-based access, and require users with temporary passwords to change them.</p>
<p><b>Driver and truck management:</b> The system shall collect driver and truck information, accept verification documents, allow administrator approval, display eligible FTL trucks, and display shared loads only after an open shared trip has been created.</p>
<p><b>Cargo and booking management:</b> The system shall allow customers to submit cargo requests, book available FTL trucks, book capacity on shared trips, prevent excessive capacity booking, and update remaining capacity automatically.</p>
<p><b>Bidding and marketplace:</b> Drivers shall be able to view eligible cargo requests and submit bids. Customers shall be able to compare bids and accept one suitable offer.</p>
<p><b>Trip management:</b> The system shall create trips from confirmed bookings, allow drivers to update trip status, and allow customers and administrators to monitor progress.</p>
<p><b>Reporting:</b> The system shall generate reports about users, drivers, trucks, cargo requests, bookings, trips, earnings, and system activities.</p>

<h3>3.7.2 Non-Functional Requirements</h3>
<ul>
  <li><b>Security:</b> Passwords shall be hashed, input validated, and access restricted according to user roles.</li>
  <li><b>Performance:</b> Pages and API requests shall respond within an acceptable time under normal use.</li>
  <li><b>Reliability:</b> Booking, capacity, and trip records shall remain consistent.</li>
  <li><b>Usability:</b> Interfaces shall be clear, responsive, and suitable for computers and mobile devices.</li>
  <li><b>Scalability:</b> The platform shall accommodate increasing numbers of users, trucks, and trips.</li>
  <li><b>Maintainability:</b> Modular components shall make future correction and enhancement easier.</li>
</ul>

<h3>3.7.3 System Features</h3>
<p>The developed system provides the following main features:</p>
<table>
  <tr><th>Feature</th><th>Description</th></tr>
  <tr><td>User Registration and Authentication</td><td>Customers and drivers create accounts using validated personal information. Driver registration also records the service type, either Full Truck Load (FTL) or Shared Load, together with vehicle and verification documents. Secure login, password recovery, email verification, account locking, and role-based access are supported.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>User Profile and Role Display</td><td>Every authenticated user can view and update a profile. The profile clearly displays the account role. A driver profile additionally displays whether the driver provides FTL or Shared Load services.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Driver and Truck Verification</td><td>Drivers submit licence, identity, truck, registration, and insurance information. An administrator reviews the submitted records before activating the driver and truck.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>FTL Truck Marketplace</td><td>Customers can view active and available full trucks, inspect vehicle information, select a suitable truck, enter pickup and delivery locations, specify cargo weight in kilograms or tons, and attach a cargo photograph.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Open FTL Requests and Bidding</td><td>A customer may publish an FTL request instead of selecting a particular truck. Eligible FTL drivers can review the request and submit price and estimated-time offers for customer approval.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Shared Load Marketplace</td><td>A Shared Load vehicle becomes bookable only after its driver creates an open shared trip. Customers can reserve part of its available capacity. The system prevents overbooking and automatically reduces the remaining capacity.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Phone-Assisted Booking</td><td>An administrator can record a booking for a customer who calls or visits the office and cannot use the online service. The page presents active, available FTL trucks and open Shared Trips. The administrator selects a suitable option and records pickup contact, pickup location, delivery contact, delivery location, cargo details, weight, and photograph.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Driver and Vehicle Selection</td><td>For an FTL phone booking, the administrator selects an active driver with an available full truck and no conflicting active trip. For a Shared phone booking, the administrator selects an open shared trip with sufficient remaining capacity.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Trip and Shipment Management</td><td>Confirmed transport activities are recorded as trips. The system stores route, customer, driver, truck, cargo, weight, booking type, price, and operational status for monitoring and reporting.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>SMS and In-System Notifications</td><td>The system can notify the driver about a new phone booking and inform pickup and delivery contacts about the selected driver, telephone number, and truck plate number.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Input Validation and Data Types</td><td>Registration and booking inputs are validated before storage. Names and short descriptions use bounded strings, telephone numbers use validated character fields, weights and capacity use positive numeric values, identifiers use UUID values, and fixed choices such as role and load type use enumerated values.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Reports and Audit Logs</td><td>Administrators can review operational summaries and reports about users, trucks, requests, shared trips, bookings, payments, and activities. Important administrative operations are recorded in audit logs.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>3.8 Feasibility Study</h2>
<h3>3.8.1 Technical Feasibility</h3>
<p>The system is technically feasible because it uses widely supported web technologies. React provides the user interface, Node.js and Express provide backend services, and PostgreSQL stores structured data.</p>
<h3>3.8.2 Operational Feasibility</h3>
<p>The system supports activities already performed by customers, drivers, and administrators while transferring them from manual communication to a centralized platform.</p>
<h3>3.8.3 Economic Feasibility</h3>
<p>Most technologies used in the project are open source, reducing software licensing costs. Cloud resources can be expanded according to actual system usage.</p>
<h3>3.8.4 Schedule Feasibility</h3>
<p>The system can be completed within the project period by dividing development into authentication, user management, truck management, marketplace, booking, trip, and reporting modules.</p>

<h2>3.9 System Architecture</h2>
<p>The system uses a three-tier architecture:</p>
<div class="diagram">
  <b>Presentation Layer</b><br>React Web Application<br><br>
  ↓ HTTPS / REST API ↓<br><br>
  <b>Application Layer</b><br>Node.js and Express<br>Authentication, Validation and Business Logic<br><br>
  ↓ Prisma ORM ↓<br><br>
  <b>Data Layer</b><br>PostgreSQL Database
</div>
<p>The presentation layer displays interfaces and collects input. The application layer performs authentication, authorization, validation, booking, bidding, trip processing, and reporting. The data layer stores users, trucks, cargo requests, bids, trips, bookings, notifications, and audit records.</p>

<h2>3.10 System Actors</h2>
<table>
  <tr><th>Actor</th><th>Main Responsibilities</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Customer</td><td>Posts cargo, browses trucks, reviews bids, books transport, and tracks shipments.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Driver</td><td>Manages truck information, reviews jobs, submits bids, creates shared trips, and updates trip progress.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Administrator</td><td>Verifies drivers, manages users and trucks, creates phone-assisted bookings, selects suitable available vehicles, monitors operations, and generates reports.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>System</td><td>Validates information, enforces permissions, updates records, and sends notifications.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>3.11 Use-Case Description</h2>
<h3>3.11.1 Customer Use Cases</h3>
<ul><li>Register and sign in</li><li>Manage profile</li><li>Browse FTL trucks and open shared trips</li><li>Post cargo requests</li><li>Review bids and book transport</li><li>Track shipments and view history</li></ul>
<h3>3.11.2 Driver Use Cases</h3>
<ul><li>Register driver and truck details</li><li>Upload verification documents</li><li>Update truck availability</li><li>Review cargo requests and submit bids</li><li>Create shared trips</li><li>Manage jobs and delivery status</li></ul>
<h3>3.11.3 Administrator Use Cases</h3>
<ul><li>Manage users and trucks</li><li>Verify drivers and documents</li><li>View available FTL trucks and open Shared Trips</li><li>Create phone-assisted bookings for customers who call or visit the office</li><li>Select a suitable driver, truck, or shared trip for a phone booking</li><li>Monitor requests, bookings, and trips</li><li>Review payments and earnings</li><li>Generate reports and review audit logs</li></ul>

<h2>3.12 Database Design</h2>
<table>
  <tr><th>Entity</th><th>Purpose</th></tr>
  <tr><td>User</td><td>Stores customer, driver, and administrator accounts.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Truck</td><td>Stores truck details and links each vehicle to its driver.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Customer Profile</td><td>Stores customer-specific information.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Cargo Request</td><td>Stores online and phone-assisted bookings, cargo, route, contacts, weight, transport requirements, booking channel, and selected vehicle information.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Bid</td><td>Stores offers submitted by drivers.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Shared Trip</td><td>Stores shared routes, schedules, prices, and available capacity.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Shared Booking</td><td>Stores customer capacity reservations.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Trip</td><td>Stores confirmed transportation activities and status.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Payment</td><td>Stores financial transaction information.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Notification</td><td>Stores messages sent to system users.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Audit Log</td><td>Records important user and administrative activities.</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Main relationships include: one driver has one truck; one customer can create many cargo requests; one cargo request can receive many bids; one driver can create many shared trips; one shared trip can receive many bookings; and one truck can participate in many trips over time.</p>
<p>Phone-assisted booking information is stored in the Cargo Request entity using a booking-channel value. The record may also store the selected driver, selected truck, administrator who completed the booking, and the date and time of selection. Registration and booking fields use suitable PostgreSQL data types, including UUID for identifiers, VARCHAR or bounded strings for names and contact details, TEXT for longer descriptions, DECIMAL for weights, capacity and prices, ENUM for controlled status and load-type values, DATE for selected dates, and TIMESTAMPTZ for audit timestamps.</p>

<h2>3.13 Tools and Technologies</h2>
<table>
  <tr><th>Technology</th><th>Purpose</th></tr>
  <tr><td>React</td><td>Frontend user-interface development</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Tailwind CSS</td><td>Responsive interface styling</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Node.js and Express</td><td>Backend and REST API development</td></tr>
  <tr><td>PostgreSQL</td><td>Relational database management</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Prisma ORM</td><td>Database schema and data access</td></tr>
  <tr><td>JSON Web Token</td><td>User authentication</td></tr>
  <tr><td>bcrypt</td><td>Password hashing</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Zod</td><td>Request and form-data validation</td></tr>
  <tr><td>React Query</td><td>Frontend server-state management</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Git and GitHub</td><td>Source-code version control</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Vitest</td><td>Automated backend testing</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Vite</td><td>Frontend development and production build</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>3.14 Security Design</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Passwords are stored using secure hashing.</li>
  <li>Protected endpoints require valid authentication.</li>
  <li>Role-based permissions restrict sensitive functions.</li>
  <li>Inputs are validated before database operations.</li>
  <li>Failed login attempts can temporarily lock accounts.</li>
  <li>Driver accounts require document verification.</li>
  <li>Administrative activities are recorded in audit logs.</li>
  <li>Database transactions protect multi-step booking operations.</li>
  <li>Rate limiting protects authentication and password-reset endpoints.</li>
</ul>

<h2>3.15 Testing Strategy</h2>
<p><b>Unit testing</b> verifies individual functions and validation rules. <b>Integration testing</b> verifies communication between API routes, business logic, and database repositories. <b>System testing</b> evaluates complete workflows such as registration, booking, bidding, shared-trip creation, and delivery. <b>User-acceptance testing</b> determines whether the completed system satisfies operational requirements and is easy to use.</p>

<h2>3.16 Test Cases</h2>
<table>
  <tr><th>Test Case</th><th>Expected Result</th></tr>
  <tr><td>User enters valid credentials</td><td>User is authenticated and redirected to the correct dashboard.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>User enters an incorrect password</td><td>An appropriate error message is displayed.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Unverified driver attempts to access jobs</td><td>Access is denied until verification.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Customer browses trucks</td><td>Eligible FTL trucks are displayed.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>SHARED driver has not created a trip</td><td>The truck does not appear as a shared load.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>SHARED driver creates an open trip</td><td>The shared load appears in the marketplace.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Customer books valid shared capacity</td><td>The booking is created and remaining capacity is reduced.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Customer requests excessive capacity</td><td>The booking is rejected.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Administrator opens Phone Bookings</td><td>Active and available FTL trucks and open Shared Trips with capacity are displayed.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Administrator creates an FTL phone booking</td><td>The customer, pickup, delivery, cargo and selected full-truck information are stored.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Administrator creates a Shared phone booking</td><td>The booking is linked to the selected open Shared Trip and its remaining capacity is reduced.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Administrator enters an invalid telephone number</td><td>The booking is rejected with a validation message.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>User opens the profile page</td><td>The account role is displayed; a driver also sees FTL or Shared service type.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Unauthorized user accesses an admin page</td><td>Access is denied.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Driver completes delivery</td><td>The trip status is updated and stored.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>3.17 Ethical Considerations</h2>
<p>Users should be informed about the personal information collected by the platform. Driver licences, identity information, telephone numbers, and vehicle documents must only be used for legitimate transportation and verification activities. Access to sensitive information should be limited to authorized users, and testing data should not expose real passwords or confidential documents.</p>

<h2>3.18 Chapter Summary</h2>
<p>This chapter presented the methodology used to develop the Smart Truck Dispatch and Cargo Marketplace Management System. It discussed data collection, existing-system limitations, proposed-system capabilities, Agile methodology, system requirements, feasibility, architecture, database design, development technologies, security controls, and testing procedures. The next chapter presents system implementation, testing results, and interface demonstrations.</p>
